      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The counter-argument that outcome equality harms high-performing applicants often overlooks the fact that \"merit\" in credit was historically calculated using biased denominators that penalized marginalized groups regardless of individual performance. By adjusting the starting line to account for systemic barriers, an equality-of-outcome model ensures that even exceptional borrowers from historically excluded communities receive the resources they actually need to succeed. If a lending model ignores these structural headwinds, it risks perpetuating the very economic disparities that ESG frameworks were designed to solve, ultimately undermining long-term financial stability for everyone.",
